hint: Halving a binary number removes its last digit; an odd number ends in 1, and adding 1 turns it into a 0 with a carry going left.
hint: Walk from the last digit to the second, with a `carry`. A digit plus the carry of 1 means odd: add 1 (making a carry) and then halve, 2 steps. Otherwise it's even: 1 step.
hint: At the first digit, a leftover carry turns 1 into 10, which needs one more halving.
right: Working from the right with a carry follows the additions without ever building the big number, and each digit costs 1 or 2 steps.
wrong: Start at the last index with `carry = 0`; while `i > 0`: if `int(s[i]) + carry == 1`, add 2 steps and set `carry = 1`, else add 1; move left; finally add `carry`.

=== task: code hard
title: Wildcard Matching
leetcode: 44 | Wildcard Matching | wildcard-matching
patterns: index + until + break
prompt: A pattern `p` can hold `?`, which matches any one character, and `*`, which matches any run of characters, even an empty one. Every other character matches only itself, and the pattern must match the whole text. `s` and `p` are already set. Create `match`.

**Example 1:** if `s` is `"adceb"` and `p` is `"*a*b"`, `match` is `True`.

**Example 2:** if `s` is `"aa"` and `p` is `"a"`, `match` is `False`: the pattern doesn't cover the whole text. `"cb"` against `"?a"` is `False` too.

**Constraints:** `s` and `p` have 0 to 2,000 characters; `s` holds small letters, and `p` small letters, `?` and `*`.
starter:
```python
# s and p are already set for you
match = False
```
solution:
```python
i = 0
j = 0
star = -1
mark = 0
match = True
while i < len(s):
    if j < len(p) and (p[j] == "?" or p[j] == s[i]):
        i += 1
        j += 1
    elif j < len(p) and p[j] == "*":
        star = j
        mark = i
        j += 1
    elif star != -1:
        j = star + 1
        mark += 1
        i = mark
    else:
        match = False
        break
while j < len(p) and p[j] == "*":
    j += 1
match = match and j == len(p)
```
tests:
- match
cases:
- s = "adceb"\np = "*a*b"
- s = "aa"\np = "a"
- s = "cb"\np = "?a"
- s = "aa"\np = "*"
- s = "acdcb"\np = "a*c?b"
- s = ""\np = "***"
- s = "abc"\np = "abc*"
fail[4]: `*` alone matches any text at all.
fail[6]: Stars can match nothing, so empty text matches `"***"`.
fail[7]: The final `*` matches the empty run after c.
hint: Walk through the text and the pattern with two indexes, `i` and `j`. A letter or `?` that matches moves both forward.
hint: At a `*`, remember where it is (`star`) and where the text was (`mark`), and first try letting it match nothing: move only `j`.
hint: When something fails after a star, go back: let the star swallow one more character (`mark += 1`, `i = mark`, `j = star + 1`). With no star to go back to, there's no match. At the end, only stars may be left in the pattern.
right: Only the latest star ever needs to grow, so the two indexes plus the star's saved position try every useful option without starting over from scratch.
wrong: Two indexes; on a match move both; on `*` save `star = j`, `mark = i` and move `j`; on a mismatch after a star, reset `j = star + 1` and move `mark` and `i` on; else no match; finally skip trailing stars and check `j == len(p)`.

=== task: code hard
title: Shortest Palindrome
leetcode: 214 | Shortest Palindrome | shortest-palindrome
patterns: until + ends
prompt: `s` is already set. Add as few characters as possible to the **front** of `s` to make a palindrome. Create `result` with that palindrome.

**Example 1:** if `s` is `"aacecaaa"`, add one a: `result` is `"aaacecaaa"`.

**Example 2:** if `s` is `"abcd"`, add d, c and b: `result` is `"dcbabcd"`.

**Constraints:** `s` has 0 to 1,000 small letters.
starter:
```python
# s is already set for you
result = ""
```
solution:
```python
k = len(s)
while s[:k] != s[:k][::-1]:
    k -= 1
result = s[k:][::-1] + s
```
tests:
- result
cases:
- s = "aacecaaa"
- s = "abcd"
- s = ""
- s = "a"
- s = "aba"
- s = "abb"
fail[5]: `"aba"` is already a palindrome: add nothing.
fail[6]: The longest palindrome at the start is `"a"`, so `"bb"` reversed goes in front: `"bbabb"`.
hint: The characters you add in front must mirror the end of `s`. The part of `s` you don't have to mirror is the longest palindrome that `s` starts with.
hint: Find it by trying the longest start first: shrink `k` from `len(s)` while `s[:k]` isn't a palindrome. One character is always a palindrome, and so is `""`, so the loop ends.
hint: Then put the rest, reversed, in front: `s[k:][::-1] + s`.
right: The longest palindromic start can stay in the middle untouched, and only the characters after it need a mirror image in front.
wrong: `k = len(s)`; `while s[:k] != s[:k][::-1]:` `k -= 1`; then `result = s[k:][::-1] + s`.

=== task: code hard
title: Number of Digit One
leetcode: 233 | Number of Digit One | number-of-digit-one
patterns: digits + until
prompt: `n` is already set. Create `ones` with the total number of digit 1s written when you write out every whole number from 0 to `n`.

**Example 1:** if `n` is `13`, the 1s are in 1, 10, 11 (two), 12 and 13: `ones` is `6`.

**Example 2:** if `n` is `0`, `ones` is `0`.

**Constraints:** `n` is from 0 to 1,000,000,000, so writing out every number would take far too long.
starter:
```python
# n is already set for you
ones = 0
```
solution:
```python
ones = 0
place = 1
while place <= n:
    high = n // (place * 10)
    digit = n // place % 10
    low = n % place
    if digit == 0:
        ones += high * place
    elif digit == 1:
        ones += high * place + low + 1
    else:
        ones += (high + 1) * place
    place = place * 10
```
tests:
- ones
cases:
- n = 13
- n = 0
- n = 99
- n = 100
- n = 1000000000
fail[3]: The ones place shows a 1 ten times (1, 11, 21, up to 91) and the tens place ten times (10 to 19): 20.
fail[4]: 100 adds one more 1 in the hundreds place: 21.
hint: Count the 1s place by place: how many numbers from 0 to `n` have a 1 in the ones place, in the tens place, and up.
hint: For one place, split `n` into the digits above it (`high`), the digit there (`digit`) and the digits below it (`low`). Each full cycle of `high` gives `place` ones.
hint: If `digit` is 0, that's all: `high * place`. If it's 1, add the partial cycle, `low + 1`. If it's 2 or more, the partial cycle is complete: `(high + 1) * place`.
right: Counting per place turns a billion numbers into ten steps, and the digit at each place decides whether the last cycle is missing, partial or complete.
wrong: `place = 1`; while `place <= n`: split into `high`, `digit` and `low`, add `high * place`, `high * place + low + 1` or `(high + 1) * place` by the digit, then `place = place * 10`.

=== task: code hard
title: Reaching Points
leetcode: 780 | Reaching Points | reaching-points
patterns: until + break
prompt: From a point (x, y), one move goes to (x + y, y) or to (x, x + y). `sx`, `sy`, `tx` and `ty` are already set. Create `reachable`: `True` when some moves lead from (`sx`, `sy`) to (`tx`, `ty`).

**Example 1:** from (1, 1) to (3, 5): (1, 1), (1, 2), (3, 2), (3, 5): `True`.

**Example 2:** from (1, 1) to (2, 2): `False`. From (1, 1) to (1, 1): `True`, with no moves.

**Constraints:** all four are from 1 to 1,000,000,000.
starter:
```python
# sx, sy, tx and ty are already set for you
reachable = False
```
solution:
```python
reachable = False
while tx >= sx and ty >= sy:
    if tx == sx and ty == sy:
        reachable = True
        break
    if tx > ty:
        if ty == sy:
            reachable = (tx - sx) % ty == 0
            break
        tx = tx % ty
    elif ty > tx:
        if tx == sx:
            reachable = (ty - sy) % tx == 0
            break
        ty = ty % tx
    else:
        break
```
tests:
- reachable
cases:
- sx = 1\nsy = 1\ntx = 3\nty = 5
- sx = 1\nsy = 1\ntx = 2\nty = 2
- sx = 1\nsy = 1\ntx = 1\nty = 1
- sx = 1\nsy = 1\ntx = 1000000000\nty = 1
- sx = 3\nsy = 7\ntx = 3\nty = 4
- sx = 9\nsy = 5\ntx = 12\nty = 8
fail[2]: (2, 2) can't come from anywhere: the point before it would have a 0.
fail[4]: Going backwards one subtraction at a time would take a billion steps: subtract as many times as fit at once with `%`.
hint: Work backwards from the target: the last move added the smaller number to the bigger one, so the point before was (`tx - ty`, `ty`) when `tx > ty`.
hint: Many subtractions in a row are one `%`: `tx = tx % ty`. But if `ty` has already reached `sy`, check instead whether the remaining gap is a whole number of `ty`s, and stop.
hint: Stop when you reach the start, drop below it, or the two numbers are equal (no move leads there).
right: Going backwards there's only ever one move to undo, `%` undoes many at once, and the checks for `sx` and `sy` stop it from jumping past the start.
wrong: Loop while `tx >= sx and ty >= sy`: done if at the start; if `tx > ty`, either check `(tx - sx) % ty == 0` when `ty == sy` or set `tx = tx % ty`; the same the other way round; stop if they're equal.

=== task: code hard
title: Consecutive Numbers Sum
leetcode: 829 | Consecutive Numbers Sum | consecutive-numbers-sum
patterns: until
prompt: `n` is already set. Create `ways` with how many ways `n` can be written as a sum of one or more consecutive positive whole numbers.

**Example 1:** if `n` is `9`, it's 9, 4 + 5 and 2 + 3 + 4: `ways` is `3`.

**Example 2:** if `n` is `5`, it's 5 and 2 + 3: `ways` is `2`.

**Constraints:** `n` is from 1 to 1,000,000,000.
starter:
```python
# n is already set for you
ways = 0
```
solution:
```python
ways = 0
k = 1
while k * (k + 1) // 2 <= n:
    if (n - k * (k + 1) // 2) % k == 0:
        ways += 1
    k += 1
```
tests:
- ways
cases:
- n = 9
- n = 5
- n = 15
- n = 1
- n = 1000000000
fail[3]: 15 is 15, 7 + 8, 4 + 5 + 6 and 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5: 4 ways.
hint: Count by how many numbers the sum has, `k`. The smallest sum of `k` numbers is 1 + 2 up to `k`, which is `k * (k + 1) // 2`; once that's above `n`, no longer sum fits.
hint: `k` numbers starting at `a + 1` add up to `k * a + k * (k + 1) // 2`. So a sum of `k` numbers works exactly when `n - k * (k + 1) // 2` divides by `k`.
hint: Loop `k` up from 1 while the smallest sum fits, counting the `k`s that work.
right: Each length `k` gives at most one sum, so checking one divisibility per length counts every way, and the loop stops after about the square root of 2n steps.
wrong: `k = 1`; `while k * (k + 1) // 2 <= n:` add 1 to `ways` when `(n - k * (k + 1) // 2) % k == 0`; then `k += 1`.

=== task: code hard
title: Building Boxes
leetcode: 1739 | Building Boxes | building-boxes
patterns: until
prompt: You must put `n` cube boxes in the corner of a room. A box can only sit on another box if all four of its side faces touch a wall or another box. `n` is already set. Create `floor` with the fewest boxes that must touch the floor.

**Example 1:** if `n` is `3`, all 3 go on the floor: `floor` is `3`.

**Example 2:** if `n` is `10`, a pyramid in the corner holds 10 boxes with 6 on the floor: `floor` is `6`.

**Constraints:** `n` is from 1 to 1,000,000,000.
starter:
```python
# n is already set for you
floor = 0
```
solution:
```python
k = 0
total = 0
while total + (k + 1) * (k + 2) // 2 <= n:
    k += 1
    total += k * (k + 1) // 2
floor = k * (k + 1) // 2
extra = 0
while total < n:
    extra += 1
    total += extra
    floor += 1
```
tests:
- floor
cases:
- n = 3
- n = 4
- n = 10
- n = 1
- n = 15
- n = 1000000000
fail[2]: 3 boxes on the floor hold a fourth on top: 3.
fail[5]: The 10-box pyramid has 6 on the floor; 5 more boxes need 3 more floor boxes (they allow 1, 2 and 3 more): 9.
hint: The best shape is a stepped pyramid in the corner. A full one with `k` layers has `k * (k + 1) // 2` boxes on the floor and holds 1 + 3 + 6, up to that, in total.
hint: First build the biggest full pyramid that fits, adding layers while the next one fits.
hint: Then add floor boxes one at a time along the edge: the 1st new one adds 1 box in total, the 2nd adds 2 (one on it), the 3rd adds 3, and onward, until `n` boxes fit.
right: A full corner pyramid is the most boxes per floor box, and each extra floor box along its edge lets one more box stack than the one before, so two loops find the answer.
wrong: Grow full layers while the next layer's triangle fits; set `floor` to the last layer's size; then add floor boxes one at a time, each adding 1, 2, 3 and more boxes, until the total reaches `n`.

=== task: code hard
title: Minimum Number of Moves to Make Palindrome
leetcode: 2193 | Minimum Number of Moves to Make Palindrome | minimum-number-of-moves-to-make-palindrome
patterns: ends + until
prompt: `s` is already set; its letters can always be rearranged into a palindrome. One move swaps two neighbouring letters. Create `moves` with the fewest moves that make `s` a palindrome.

**Example 1:** if `s` is `"aabb"`, two moves make `"abba"`: `moves` is `2`.

**Example 2:** if `s` is `"letelt"`, `moves` is `2`.

**Constraints:** `s` has 1 to 2,000 small letters.
starter:
```python
# s is already set for you
moves = 0
```
solution:
```python
moves = 0
while len(s) > 2:
    c = s[0]
    j = len(s) - 1 - s[::-1].find(c)
    if j == 0:
        moves += len(s) // 2
        s = s[1:]
    else:
        moves += len(s) - 1 - j
        s = s[1:j] + s[j + 1:]
```
tests:
- moves
cases:
- s = "aabb"
- s = "letelt"
- s = "a"
- s = "abcba"
- s = "aab"
- s = "baa"
fail[4]: `"abcba"` is already a palindrome: 0.
fail[6]: The lonely b belongs in the middle: one move makes `"aba"`.
hint: Work from the outside in. The first letter's partner should end up last: find the last copy of `s[0]` (`len(s) - 1 - s[::-1].find(c)`) and count the swaps to move it to the end.
hint: Then both are in place: remove them and repeat on what's left.
hint: If the first letter has no other copy, it's the odd one that belongs in the middle: moving it there costs `len(s) // 2` swaps. Count that, set it aside, and carry on with the rest.
right: Fixing the outer pair first never costs extra, because the moves inside don't disturb it, so one pass from the outside in adds up the fewest swaps.
wrong: While `len(s) > 2`: find the last copy of `s[0]`; if it's `s[0]` itself, add `len(s) // 2` and drop it; else add the swaps to move it to the end and remove both letters.

=== task: code hard
title: Add Up the Numbers in a Text
patterns: index + continue
prompt: `text` holds letters, digits and symbols, and it's already set. Every run of digits is a whole number; a `-` directly in front of a run makes that number negative. Create `total` with the sum of all the numbers.

**Example 1:** if `text` is `"a12b-3c4"`, the numbers are 12, -3 and 4: `total` is `13`.

**Example 2:** if `text` is `"1-2-3"`, they're 1, -2 and -3: `total` is `-4`. In `"3.14"`, the dot separates 3 and 14: `17`.

**Constraints:** `text` has 0 to 1,000 characters.
starter:
```python
# text is already set for you
total = 0
```
solution:
```python
total = 0
i = 0
while i < len(text):
    if not text[i].isdigit():
        i += 1
        continue
    j = i
    while j < len(text) and text[j].isdigit():
        j += 1
    number = int(text[i:j])
    if i > 0 and text[i - 1] == "-":
        number = -number
    total += number
    i = j
```
tests:
- total
cases:
- text = "a12b-3c4"
- text = "1-2-3"
- text = "3.14"
- text = "abc"
- text = "--5"
- text = ""
fail[1]: 12 is one number, not 1 and 2: read the whole run of digits.
fail[5]: Only the `-` right in front of the digits counts: -5.
hint: Walk with an index. Anything that isn't a digit is skipped: move on and `continue`.
hint: At a digit, move a second index to the end of the run of digits; the number is `int(text[i:j])`.
hint: If the character right before the run is `-`, the number is negative. Add it, then jump `i` to `j`.
right: `continue` passes over everything that isn't a number, the inner loop reads each run whole, and one look back finds the minus sign.
wrong: `i = 0`; skip non-digits with `continue`; at a digit, find the end `j` of the run, take `int(text[i:j])`, negate it if `text[i - 1] == "-"`, add it, and set `i = j`.

=== task: code hard
title: A Beautiful Year
patterns: forever + break
prompt: A year is **beautiful** when its four digits are all different, like 2013. `year` is already set. Create `beautiful` with the first beautiful year after it. (This problem comes from the Codeforces practice site.)

**Example 1:** if `year` is `1987`, `beautiful` is `2013`.

**Example 2:** if `year` is `2013`, `beautiful` is `2014`. If `year` is `1000`, it's `1023`.

**Constraints:** `year` is from 1000 to 9000, so the answer always has four digits.
starter:
```python
# year is already set for you
beautiful = 0
```
solution:
```python
beautiful = year
while True:
    beautiful += 1
    digits = str(beautiful)
    different = True
    for ch in digits:
        if digits.count(ch) > 1:
            different = False
    if different:
        break
```
tests:
- beautiful
cases:
- year = 1987
- year = 2013
- year = 1000
- year = 8999
- year = 2999
fail[2]: The answer must come after `year`, even when `year` is beautiful itself.
fail[4]: 9000 to 9011 all repeat a digit; 9012 doesn't.
hint: Try the years after `year` one by one: `while True:`, add 1, check, `break` when it's beautiful.
hint: A year has four different digits when none of them appears more than once: `digits.count(ch) > 1` spots a repeat.
hint: Check every digit with a `for` loop inside the `while`, and break out of the `while` only when no digit repeats.
right: There's no way to know how many years to try, so the loop runs until the check passes, and the inner loop checks each digit of each year.
wrong: `while True:` add 1 to the year; check each digit's count with a `for` loop; `break` when none repeats.

=== task: code hard
title: Long Division
patterns: until + digits
prompt: `a`, `b` and `k` are already set, with `b` above 0. Create `decimals` with the first `k` digits after the decimal point of `a / b`, as text, worked out exactly the way long division does, without floats (which keep only about 16 digits).

**Example 1:** if `a` is `1`, `b` is `7` and `k` is `12`, `decimals` is `"142857142857"`.

**Example 2:** if `a` is `22`, `b` is `7` and `k` is `6`, 22 / 7 is 3.142857, so `decimals` is `"142857"`. If `a` is `1`, `b` is `8` and `k` is `6`, it's `"125000"`.

**Constraints:** `a` is from 0 to 1,000,000, `b` from 1 to 1,000,000, and `k` from 0 to 1,000.
starter:
```python
# a, b and k are already set for you
decimals = ""
```
solution:
```python
decimals = ""
rem = a % b
while len(decimals) < k:
    rem = rem * 10
    decimals += str(rem // b)
    rem = rem % b
```
tests:
- decimals
cases:
- a = 1\nb = 7\nk = 12
- a = 22\nb = 7\nk = 6
- a = 1\nb = 8\nk = 6
- a = 5\nb = 5\nk = 3
- a = 2\nb = 3\nk = 0
- a = 1\nb = 999983\nk = 40
fail[3]: After 125 the division comes out exactly, so the rest of the digits are 0s.
fail[4]: 5 / 5 is exactly 1: the decimals are all 0.
hint: The whole part doesn't matter: start from the remainder `a % b`.
hint: Each decimal digit is found the long-division way: multiply the remainder by 10, the digit is `rem // b`, and the new remainder is `rem % b`.
hint: Repeat until you have `k` digits.
right: Long division only ever needs the remainder, which stays below `b`, so every digit comes out exact however far you go.
wrong: `rem = a % b`; while fewer than `k` digits: `rem = rem * 10`, add `str(rem // b)`, `rem = rem % b`.
